ad -2; sed -n 13,14p Outline.md | cut -c1-1200; cd /hutian/repo && forge api 'repos/world/story-repo/issues/8/comments' | head -c 1500"]], "output": "```console\nfcd0f14 Outline rev. 12: OLB training-side rule result in C4\nb11eec7 Outline rev. 11: OLB results (C7 branch), C4 conditional, title decision, Table 3 verdict, figures as built\n4. We give a closed-form risk for the factor model (floor + design term + residual) that attributes the error, and test on synthetic data (known truth) and on the real matrix whether a sufficiency rule k*(ε) read off the spectrum carries; on real data it does not; on the primary (common-target) score the design term is predicted well (T1 is conservative for it), but the floor term, which dominates from k ≈ 8, is about 2.5× what the factor model predicts, and the cause of that misfit is not identified (Brief rev. 10); T1 is a decomposition of the design part, not a predictor of the floor.\n5. Main result (numbers from the common-target-set rerun): the error-vs-k curve on held-out groups; the k that gets within ε points of the floor, for a range of ε; designed selection matters when k is very small (≤ 5) and random-k is within noise from about k = 8 at the coarse scale; beyond that the error sits in the floor, which is larger than the factor model predicts; that better inference rather than better selection would lower it is a hypothesis, carried as a claim only if Table 3 shows an inference rule lowering the excess over the floor by more than δ_floor (part of the residual may be the floor itself), and no k resolves neighbouring models (floor ≈ 5 points on the common target set; the pre-set margin of about 0.1 point, set by adjacent-model gaps, is not reached at any k).\n[]\n```", "exit": null}}}, {"id": "0c97c0a1-a9c2-533a-9661-016bf0edcfc4", "at": 1795738552.7557673, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 66934, "layer": "worker", "category": "message", "kind": "session.session/request_permission", "role": "idea-review-re